WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:03.211
- What is a meme, if
not a joke persevering?

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.005
Sound vaguely familiar?

00:00:05.005 --> 00:00:08.383
What is x, if not y
persevering is a templated meme

00:00:08.383 --> 00:00:09.801
stemming from the poignant

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.886
or cheesy line from "WandaVision."

00:00:17.559 --> 00:00:19.811
- People took that
template and ran with it.

00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:22.772
Templates like that began
as formulaic cliches.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:24.024
"30 is the new 20,"

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:25.608
"Whatever floats your boat,"

00:00:25.608 --> 00:00:28.028
and now have become culturally pervasive.

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.655
From public service
announcements to TV shows

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:33.199
to city taglines and
even political slogans,

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:34.743
they're everywhere.

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:36.953
But what are they exactly?

00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:40.540
These adaptable cliche
frames are called snowclones.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.876
They might look a little
bit like algebra problem,

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.753
solving for x and y,

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:47.338
but this is no y equals mx + b situation.

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:49.674
It's more like Mad Libs.

00:00:49.674 --> 00:00:50.884
Let's give it a try.

00:00:50.884 --> 00:00:52.552
Not me. Hmm.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.098
Not me asking my little
sister about TikTok trends

00:00:57.098 --> 00:00:59.267
because I'm already out of touch.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:03.354
Not me using a snowclone to
try to explain snowclones.

00:01:03.354 --> 00:01:06.608
It's giving millennial
attempting to appeal to Gen Zers.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:08.568
It's giving trying too hard to be funny.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.403
It's giving Cher?

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:12.489
It's giving is a popular snowclone

00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:15.492
that became even more ubiquitous
when Shawn Mendes used it

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:18.078
in a now viral video from late 2021.

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:19.037
- It's giving.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:20.371
It's giving Cher.

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:22.165
- With celebrities like Lizzo joining in.

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:24.626
Even more interesting, there
was a homophone pun shift

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:27.962
from Cher, the Goddess of
Pop, to share the verb.

00:01:27.962 --> 00:01:30.840
And speaking of homophone pun snowclones,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.969
I would be remiss not to mention
how I'm a big fan of Dune,

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:37.180
Dune the absolute most
for the smallest laughs.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.265
I could go on and I will.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.558
So hold onto your bucket hats

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.435
because we're about to word-nerd

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.646
like we've never word-nerded before.

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:47.690
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,
and this is "Otherwords."

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.276
(light music)

00:01:55.156 --> 00:01:58.201
- [Announcer] "Otherwords."

00:01:58.201 --> 00:02:00.912
- In a 2003 blog post on Language Log,

00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:03.581
linguist Jeffrey K Pullum solicited ideas

00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:07.293
for the linguistic phenomenon
of a multi-use, customizable,

00:02:07.293 --> 00:02:09.087
instantly recognizable, time-worn,

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:11.005
quoted or misquoted phrase or sentence

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:12.924
that can be used in an entirely open array

00:02:12.924 --> 00:02:15.343
of different variants. (gasps)

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:17.887
In response, economists
Glen Whitman bestowed

00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:20.723
upon us the neologism snowclone,

00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:22.725
derived from the journalistic
cliches referring

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:25.186
to the number of Yupik
and Inuit words for snow,

00:02:25.186 --> 00:02:28.481
and, of course, combined
with a pun on snow cones.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:30.984
So the term snowclone is pretty recent,

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:33.486
but the concept goes back
further than you might think.

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:36.364
Julius Caesar is known
for a number of things

00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:37.198
though the first one

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:39.826
that comes to mind is
probably getting assassinated,

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.536
which sticks in our collective imaginary

00:02:41.536 --> 00:02:46.124
due to "Beware the Ides of
March," and "Et tu, Brute,"

00:02:46.124 --> 00:02:48.543
courtesy of, you guessed it, Shakespeare.

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:50.461
However, besides getting got,

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:51.421
Julius Caesar was remembered

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:53.756
for the phrase "Veni, vidi, vici,"

00:02:53.756 --> 00:02:56.009
I came, I saw, I conquered.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.888
And now as a snowclone, I came,
I saw, I x'ed we see plays

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:02.265
in both the English and
original Latin versions,

00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:05.393
like veni, vidi, Visa, I
came, I saw, I shopped,

00:03:05.393 --> 00:03:08.688
or veni, vidi, Velcro, I
came, I saw, I stuck around.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.232
And, of course, it's heavily
used in all sorts of media,

00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:13.610
from album titles to movies.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.321
- We came, we saw, we kicked its (beep).

00:03:16.321 --> 00:03:18.114
- [Erica] When our old
pal William Shakespeare

00:03:18.114 --> 00:03:20.783
penned his most famous
and most quotable line,

00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:22.869
"To be or not to be,"

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:24.787
he probably didn't
realize that it was poised

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:27.582
to become one of the most
recognizable cliche phrases

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:29.167
of the next few centuries.

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:32.170
- To swing or not to swing.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:36.591
(gentle music)

00:03:36.591 --> 00:03:38.343
- And Shakespeare will be Shakespeare,

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:40.511
so, of course, he does have a few more.

00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:42.263
"Much ado about nothing."

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:44.349
"Frailty thy name is woman."

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:46.142
"My kingdom for a horse."

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:47.936
"A rose by any other name."

00:03:47.936 --> 00:03:49.687
In his 1637 philosophical

00:03:49.687 --> 00:03:52.941
and autobiographical treatise
"Discourse on the Method,"

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:56.236
Rene Decartes wrote, "I
think, therefore I am,"

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:57.654
or in modern terms,

00:03:57.654 --> 00:04:00.448
I don't exactly think so I ain't exactly.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:02.867
While the phrase itself
is commonly repeated,

00:04:02.867 --> 00:04:04.285
it is often snowcloned

00:04:04.285 --> 00:04:07.789
such as in the 2015 film
"I Eat, Therefore I Am,"

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:09.249
or the two books on Goodreads

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:11.125
called "I Drink, Therefore I Am,"

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:13.586
one a wine guide and one about sobriety.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:15.505
Philosophy is a funny thing, isn't it?

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:16.881
During the 19th century,

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:19.175
eight-year-old Virginia
O'Hanlon wrote to the editors

00:04:19.175 --> 00:04:22.512
of the New York Sun to ask
about the existence of Santa.

00:04:22.512 --> 00:04:25.265
So from editor Francis
Pharcellus Church's response,

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:27.475
"Yes, Virginia, there is a Santa Claus,"

00:04:27.475 --> 00:04:29.644
we get both yes, x, there is a y,

00:04:29.644 --> 00:04:31.271
and a little Christmas magic.

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:33.314
Another popular snowclone comes

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:36.150
with the 1939 film "The Wizard of Oz,"

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.152
where Dorothy and her friends are fearful

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:40.738
of what they might face
in the Haunted Forest.

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:43.116
"Lions and tigers and bears, oh my!"

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:45.118
It has since been adopted by journalists

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:48.329
as a prime cliche for
headlines of all sorts.

00:04:48.329 --> 00:04:49.747
In the late 1960s,

00:04:49.747 --> 00:04:52.750
"Star Trek" gave us a
special sort of snowclone.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.627
Dr. McCoy says to Captain Kirk,

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:59.048
"I'm a doctor, not an engineer,
or mechanic, or bricklayer."

00:04:59.048 --> 00:05:01.426
In the I'm an x, not a y format,

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:04.679
Dr. McCoy fills in the y
as the context demands.

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:07.140
Most snowclones originate
as a consistent phrase

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.599
that has been modified,

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:10.810
but here it's snowclones all the way down.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.479
The rise of the internet in meme culture

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:16.232
has caused an avalanche in snowclones,

00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:18.526
from alternate words and NFTs

00:05:18.526 --> 00:05:20.695
to engagement announcement parodies.

00:05:20.695 --> 00:05:23.865
I mean, how else do you
explain Boaty McBoatface

00:05:23.865 --> 00:05:25.033
being the number one choice

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:27.452
when the British public
named a polar research ship?

00:05:27.452 --> 00:05:30.079
And snowclones aren't limited to words.

00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:32.999
In the right context, shapes
can fill in the blanks.

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:35.084
The iconic I Heart New York logo,

00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:37.462
created by graphic designer Milton Glaser,

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:41.090
has been adapted into an
I, shape, x snowclone.

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:43.801
Sometimes it's still a
heart and another location,

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.512
but oftentimes we can see
some creative license,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:50.558
like I food cart Street
Food, or I shamrock Guinness.

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.518
So why use snowclones?

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:53.728
In one simple phrase,

00:05:53.728 --> 00:05:56.647
you're showing affiliation with
others who use the snowclone

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:58.232
through a digital inside joke

00:05:58.232 --> 00:06:00.610
that is a shared piece of popular culture.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:02.195
They're efficient forms of communication

00:06:02.195 --> 00:06:04.238
for those who understand the context,

00:06:04.238 --> 00:06:07.617
and oftentimes they're funny,
which is just an added bonus.

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:09.911
Put simply, humans crave connection,

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:12.288
and memes just do it for us.

00:06:12.288 --> 00:06:14.248
And hey, if using snowclones is wrong,

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:16.459
I don't wanna be right.

00:06:16.459 --> 00:06:17.418
Before you go,

00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:20.463
our friends over on It's
Lit! are launching a podcast.

00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:22.298
Hosted by the amazing Princess Weekes,

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:24.300
"It's Lit! [Unabridged]" features

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.135
all your favorite literature topics

00:06:26.135 --> 00:06:29.305
and thoughtful funny conversations
from amazing authors.

00:06:29.305 --> 00:06:31.808
It's basically like joining
the best book club ever

00:06:31.808 --> 00:06:34.185
but without actually
having to read anything.

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:35.520
Check out the It's Lit! podcast

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.605
in the link in the description below.

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:38.606
Although the first one

00:06:38.606 --> 00:06:40.566
that probably comes to mind is getting...

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.318
I said... (babbles)

00:06:42.318 --> 00:06:44.570
Because we're about to
word-learned like...

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:45.822
Word-learned?

00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:47.865
And Shakespeare. (stammers)

00:06:47.865 --> 00:06:49.409
Damn it, Jim, I'm not a doctor.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:50.910
I'm sorry, I am a doctor.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.454
